1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,028
Fritz Haber's relationship with that war went further still.

2
00:00:04,028 --> 00:00:06,937
In April 1915, at the Second Battle of Ypres,

3
00:00:06,937 --> 00:00:12,303
Haber personally oversaw the first large-scale chemical weapons attack in history —

4
00:00:12,303 --> 00:00:15,341
chlorine gas, released against Allied trenches.

5
00:00:15,341 --> 00:00:18,637
The same mind that helped make bread out of air had

6
00:00:18,637 --> 00:00:22,516
helped turn the battlefield into something new and horrific.

7
00:00:22,516 --> 00:00:24,714
In 1918, with the war barely over,

8
00:00:24,714 --> 00:00:27,622
Haber received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry —

9
00:00:27,622 --> 00:00:30,531
for the nitrogen-fixation work, not the gas —

10
00:00:30,531 --> 00:00:35,185
a decision that was bitterly controversial around the world at the time.

11
00:00:35,185 --> 00:00:38,935
Carl Bosch received his own, separate Nobel Prize in 1931,

12
00:00:38,935 --> 00:00:42,619
for the industrial engineering that made it all possible.

